# gitleaks baseline — accepted findings, one fingerprint per line.
#
# CI's "Security scan" job runs `gitleaks detect` over the FULL history
# (.github/workflows/ci.yml, checkout with fetch-depth: 0), so a finding is
# attributed to the commit that introduced the line and stays reportable
# forever — rewriting history is not on the table. This file is how a reviewed,
# accepted finding stops failing the build, and it is the only sanctioned way:
# the scan itself is never disabled, downgraded to a warning, or narrowed by an
# allowlist. (An allowlist was measured and rejected — see below.)
#
# Format: <commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>, from the Fingerprint field of
#   gitleaks detect --report-format json
# Comment lines are ignored by the parser.
#
# ── The rule for adding to this file ──────────────────────────────────────
# A new fingerprint goes in ONLY after a human has read the flagged line and
# confirmed it is not a credential. Never append one to make a red build green
# — that is the same trap tests/KNOWN_FAILURES.md warns about, and it converts
# the secret scan into decoration. If the value IS a real secret: rotate it
# first, then remove it, and only then discuss the fingerprint.
#
# ── What is accepted here (all 43 entries) ────────────────────────────────
# Every entry is rule `generic-api-key` — gitleaks' catch-all heuristic — firing
# on hardcoded PASSWORD FIXTURES in Go auth/usermgmt tests: request bodies of
# the form {"currentPassword": "<16-char mixed-case literal>"}, as in
# change_password_test.go. They match the rule's `password…=…<10+ chars>` shape
# and clear its entropy floor of 3.5, so they read as secrets to a regex. They
# are literals in _test.go files, compiled into the test binary and used against
# an ephemeral test database. (No fixture value is reproduced here — see the
# note below on key-shaped strings in this file.)
#
# They come from three commits, all from July 2026:
#   71461a7 (2026-07-10) Security hardening, v1.0.2 changelog release …  30
#   df49d80 (2026-07-11) Add self-service password reset and Jira CSV …   7
#   48dcf89 (2026-07-18) updates                                          6
#
# ── Why a config allowlist was NOT used instead ───────────────────────────
# The obvious alternative is .gitleaks.toml with
#   [allowlist] paths = ['''.*_test\.go$''']
# It was tried against gitleaks 8.18.4 and rejected on evidence: a global
# allowlist's `paths` and `regexes` are OR'd, not AND'd, so a path entry
# exempts the file from EVERY rule. Verified by planting AWS's own published
# example access-key id in a _test.go file and scanning a throwaway clone: with
# this baseline it is still caught as `aws-access-token`; with that allowlist it
# is dropped silently. The literal is deliberately NOT reproduced here — a
# key-shaped string in this file is itself a finding, which is how that was
# found out. Scoping the allowlist to the one noisy rule would mean restating
# gitleaks' own generic-api-key rule, whose stopword list is ~1480 lines and
# would drift from upstream on every version bump. A per-finding baseline keeps
# all ~150 provider rules live everywhere, including in test files.


